  "id": "COMPOUND:11458 - PROTEIN:5839",
  "verbalization": "Flumedroxone is implicated in the pathogenesis of U251 through a series of mechanistic links involving members of the SDR family of dehydrogenase/reductase enzymes. The drug targets these enzymes, which are involved in the metabolic pathways of various compounds, including 5,7-dimethoxy-8-[(E)-3-oxobut-1-enyl]chromen-2-one, 3-(3,4-dihydroxy-2-methoxyphenyl)-7-hydroxychromen-4-one, and Ergosta-4,24(28)-dien-3-one. These compounds are all known to interact with U251, a glioblastoma cell line, suggesting a potential role in tumor biology. The consistent targeting of SDR family members by Flumedroxone, coupled with the direct interaction with U251, implies that the drug may exert its effects through a mechanism involving the inhibition of metabolic pathways that are critical for cell survival and proliferation in U251 cells. The evidence supports a plausible, biologically relevant relationship between Flumedroxone and U251, particularly through the modulation of metabolic enzymes that influence cellular processes in the tumor.",
